# Database migrations

Alembic versions the four operational tables owned by this application. LangGraph
checkpoint tables are managed separately by the selected checkpoint backend.

The CLI reads migration paths and post-write hooks from `pyproject.toml`, logging
configuration from `alembic.ini`, and the database URL from `DATABASE_URL`.
Use an async SQLAlchemy URL such as `postgresql+psycopg://...` or
`sqlite+aiosqlite:///...`.

## Normal workflow

```bash
uv run alembic heads
uv run alembic upgrade head
uv run alembic current
uv run alembic check
```

Create a revision only after changing `Base.metadata`:

```bash
uv run --extra dev alembic revision --autogenerate -m "describe the schema change"
```

The development extra supplies Ruff for the post-write lint and format hooks.

Always review the generated operations, then verify both directions on a disposable
database:

```bash
uv run alembic downgrade -1
uv run alembic upgrade head
```

PostgreSQL uses JSONB for JSON documents. SQLite uses JSON and batch mode so it remains
useful for local and migration tests.

## Existing databases created with `create_all()`

The base revision intentionally mirrors the old `create_all()` schema. Do not stamp
blindly: first back up the database and verify that the four legacy tables, columns,
indexes, and constraints match `e4d7a9136c84`. Then adopt the legacy schema and run the
JSON-to-JSONB/index upgrade:

```bash
uv run alembic stamp e4d7a9136c84
```

Before upgrading PostgreSQL, validate every legacy value can be cast:

```sql
SELECT count(plan::jsonb) FROM daily_plans;
SELECT count(payload::jsonb) FROM domain_events;
SELECT count(payload::jsonb) FROM inbound_events;
SELECT count(buttons::jsonb) FROM outbox;
```

Back up first. JSONB normalizes representation: duplicate object keys collapse to the
last value, and whitespace/key order are not retained. Some JSON values that PostgreSQL
accepted textually can also fail the stricter JSONB conversion; a failed revision rolls
back transactionally. The type changes rewrite the PostgreSQL tables and take exclusive
locks, so schedule a maintenance window for non-trivial legacy datasets.

When the preflight succeeds, apply and verify the conversion:

```bash
uv run alembic upgrade head
uv run alembic check
```

The second revision uses explicit `USING ...::jsonb` expressions. SQLite keeps JSON and
receives only the new indexes.

`Database.create_schema()` is intentionally limited to SQLite. PostgreSQL environments
must be upgraded through Alembic.
